%T A033155 1559168,3916960,9769072,24321552,60199464,148803824,366051864,
%U A033155 899559584,2201636848,5384254000,13121348672,31957730688,77595810512,188324523584,455821457552,1102901174992
%N A033155 Number of n-step self-avoiding walks on the square lattice having exactly 1 pair of nonconsecutive vertices that are adjacent in the lattice.
%C A033155 Previous name was: Configurations of linear chains for a square lattice.
%C A033155 From _Petros Hadjicostas_, Jan 03 2019: (Start)
%C A033155 In the notation of Nemirovsky et al. (1992), a(n), the n-th term of the current sequence, is C_{n,m} with m=1 (and d=2). Here, for a d-dimensional hypercubic lattice, C_{n,m} is "the number of configurations of an n-bond self-avoiding chain with m neighbor contacts."
%C A033155 The terms a(1)-a(11) are given in Table I (p. 1088) in the paper by Nemirovsky et al. (1992). Using Eqs. (5) and (7b) in the paper, we have C_{n,m=1} = 2^1*1!*Bin(2,1)*p_{n,m=1}^{(1)} + 2^2*2!*Bin(2,2)*p_{n,m=1}^{(2)} = 0 + 8*p_{n,m=1}^{(2)} = 8*A038747(n). [Clarified by _Carlo Corti_, Aug 05 2026]
%C A033155 (End)
%C A033155 The terms a(12)-a(21) are obtained from Table B1 (pp. 4738-4739) of the published version of Bennett-Wood et al. (1998), corresponding to Table 4 (pp. 26-28) in Appendix B of the arXiv version. In the authors' notation, c_n(k) is the number of n-step self-avoiding walks with k contacts, and the table gives c_n(k)/4; hence a(n) = c_n(1) is four times the value tabulated for k=1. - _Petros Hadjicostas_, Jan 04 2019 [Clarified by _Carlo Corti_, Aug 05 2026]
%C A033155 The ancillary file sq_isaw_counts.ser accompanying Beaton, Guttmann, and Jensen (2020) contains the exact values c_{n,m}, the number of n-step self-avoiding walks on the square lattice with m nearest-neighbor contacts, for n <= 59. In this notation, a(n) = c_{n,1}. - _Carlo Corti_, Aug 10 2026
%H A033155 Carlo Corti, <a href="/A033155/b033155.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..59</a>
%H A033155 N. R. Beaton, A. J. Guttmann, and I. Jensen, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05852">Two-dimensional interacting self-avoiding walks: new estimates for critical temperatures and exponents</a>, arXiv:1911.05852 [cond-mat.stat-mech], 2020.
%H A033155 D. Bennett-Wood, I. G. Enting, D. S. Gaunt, A. J. Guttmann, J. L. Leask, A. L. Owczarek, and S. G. Whittington, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/0305-4470/31/20/010">Exact enumeration study of free energies of interacting polygons and walks in two dimensions</a>, J. Phys. A: Math. Gen. 31 (1998), 4725-4741.
%H A033155 D. Bennett-Wood, I. G. Enting, D. S. Gaunt, A. J. Guttmann, J. L. Leask, A. L. Owczarek, and S. G. Whittington, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/9803359">Exact enumeration study of free energies of interacting polygons and walks in two dimensions</a>, arXiv:cond-mat/9803359 [cond-mat.stat-mech], 1998.
%H A033155 M. E. Fisher and B. J. Hiley, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1731729">Configuration and free energy of a polymer molecule with solvent interaction</a>, J. Chem. Phys., 34 (1961), 1253-1267.
%H A033155 Sean A. Irvine, <a href="https://github.com/archmageirvine/joeis/blob/master/src/irvine/oeis/a033/A033155.java">Java program</a> (github)
%H A033155 A. M. Nemirovsky, K. F. Freed, T. Ishinabe, and J. F. Douglas, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01049010">Marriage of exact enumeration and 1/d expansion methods: lattice model of dilute polymers</a>, J. Statist. Phys., 67 (1992), 1083-1108; see Eq. 5 (p. 1090) and Eq. 7b (p. 1093).
%F A033155 a(n) = 8*A038747(n) for n >= 1. (It can be proved using Eqs. (5) and (7b) in the paper by Nemirovsky et al. (1992).) - _Petros Hadjicostas_, Jan 03 2019
%e A033155 From _Carlo Corti_, Aug 02 2026: (Start)
%e A033155 a(3) = 8 counts the following walk and its 7 other images under rotations and reflections. S marks the start, X the end, and the dotted segment the unique contact; the dotted segment is not a step of the walk.
%e A033155     o---o
%e A033155     |   |
%e A033155     S...X (End)
%Y A033155 Cf. A033323, A038747.
